== 3. Blocked set (12) — canonical missing, do NOT touch ==

openOODA-core: mcp (canon ooda-mcp missing), lsp (canon ooda-lsp missing)
openOODA-tools: oomcp, oomcprun (canon oomcp-cli missing)
openOODA-apps: oovm-ui (oohostmon), oodocs, oosheet, oodeck, oonotes,
               oopdf (oooffice)
openOODA-agents: oooda-engine, ooplanner (canon ooooda missing)
These absorbed names keep serving until their canonical repo is created
and gated; archiving them now would orphan the tokens.

== 4. Standard gates per wave (all must pass before any archive) ==

G1 canonical-live: canonical repo exists on github and a built binary's
   --list-personas (or persona registry page) lists every absorbed token.
G2 content-parity: absorbed code is present in the canonical tree (probe:
   gh api contents of the vendored dir or the persona dispatch table).
G3 catalog zero double-ship: no standalone package entry for the absorbed
   name in openOODA/catalog/index (persona-pointer entries are fine).
G4 drain: retiring repo has no open PRs and no commits ahead of what the
   canonical already absorbed (tip SHAs recorded, not fetched).
G5 ledger: before archiving, record org/name, tip SHA, default branch,
   last-pushed timestamp into a ledger file committed with the wave.
G6 manager sign-off per wave — one wave per approval, like KONSOLIDIERUNG.

== 5. Wave order + rollback ==

W1 small orgs (44): core/ooda [T], OS 9+2 cross, DE 12+1 cross, apps 3,
   cloud 10, agents 7. Lowest risk, mechanical, proves the gate pipeline.
W2 kernel rename-sources (16): T mode mandatory — tombstone README points
   to the canonical repo+persona. G2 vendoring verify is the load-bearing
   gate here (KONSOLIDIERUNG is the authoritative content map).
W3 tools bulk (96): A mode; same gates, batched ledger rows.
W4 blocked (12): only after canonical creation lands and passes G1/G2.

Per-repo archive step (after gates): record ledger line -> [T mode] push
tombstone commit "RETIRED: absorbed into <org>/<canon> (<persona>)" on the
default branch -> gh repo archive <org>/<name> -> confirm isArchived=true
-> ledger line marked done. Zero deletes, zero force-pushes anywhere.

Rollback: gh repo unarchive <org>/<name> restores the full repo (history,
branches, settings) — archive is fully reversible. A tombstone commit is
undone by a normal revert commit against the recorded tip SHA (no history
rewrite; manager rejected force-push precedent stands). Local checkouts
under /Projects/<org>/<name> are untouched by remote archive; optional
cleanup is a separate later step, out of scope here.
